      "side": 1,
      "message": "Eric, if you have a chance, could you please provide early feedback on the interface, before I proceed to implementation.\n\nPlease note that I have considered non-blocking input as well, although currently we don\u0027t have corresponding flag at the HAL level (system/hardware/interfaces/media/aidl/android/media/audio/common/AudioInputFlags.aidl). However, this mode might make sense when buffers are large. In this case, the HAL unblocks the client from a read operation before the data is actually available, and notifies it via a callback when the data has been obtained.\n\nAlso note that when the h/w is not consuming (for output) or producing (for input), the I/O operation is in fact just copying of the data from / to the ring buffer, and thus is always synchronous since it completes quickly. I\u0027ve made a comment about that in state machines.",
      "revId": "30a55d7ced1d39cf867e897cb844d7cf304e3873",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6a950a80_d5e99451",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/android/hardware/audio/core/IStreamCallback.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-10T09:38:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is the amount of data that has just been freed by the driver (in case of playback) right? Not the amount of data that was consumed from the latest write attempt.\nBecause the first sentence \"Indicate that the burst of data from the last asynchronous I/O operation has been produced (for input) or consumed (for output)\" is misleading for me: the data consumed is not from the latest burst but from an older burst which was at the head of the driver buffer, not at the tail as the latest burst.\nso the callback is not relative to the latest write burst. It is just an indication that more room is available to write data (or data available to read for input)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 31,
        "startChar": 46,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 20
      },
